ali: ping — Solaris onboarding showing a burst of 429s right after ephemeral key rotate. attaching short trace + their init pattern.
ali: trace:
```
2026-04-18T09:12:10Z WARN  token exchange status=429 path=/v1/tokens/ephemeral origin=cloud-fn-12
2026-04-18T09:12:11Z ERROR request failed status=429 path=/v1/chat/completions client=solar-devs
2026-04-18T09:12:11Z WARN  concurrent exchanges=96 origin=cloud-fn-*-eu
```
ali: their onboarding harness spins up ~96 short-lived cloud functions that each fetch ephemeral then immediately call chat. they are using rust-sdk v0.2.0 with the SDK default init flow.
rahul: alert-bot posted anomaly :alert-bot:
alert-bot: [alert] rate_limit_burst account=solaris region=eu-west window=00:00:10 peak_rps=260 baseline_rps=22 id=rl-20260418-61
morgan: pattern looks like rotate-triggered fanout. quick mitigation plan: 1) ask Solaris to front ephemeral exchange with a tiny broker/proxy that caches identical requests for 1-2s; 2) add a very small concurrency cap in their function warmup (<=8) and jittered backoff for 429s; 3) we can open a short 90s elevated priority window so in-flight calls can succeed while they patch.
ali: they fetch ephemeral directly from cloud functions — no broker. i pasted their init snippet (Rust):
```rust
let client = Redwood::new(env::var("EPH_KEY").unwrap());
// each function runs this immediately on start
let r = client.chat().create(&req).await?;
```
rahul: infra notes: enable preflight coalescing for account=solaris when concurrent preflight > 50 in 12s. coalesce window=120s. that will dedupe identical ephemeral/token checks. i'm standing by to apply the rule if CS approves.
morgan: customer comms i'll send: "we observed a short-lived surge when your keys rotated. short-term: deploy a lightweight token broker or add a local 2s cache in your functions and cap concurrent init to 8 with jittered exponential backoff. we'll open a 90s priority window and can enable short coalescing on our side to let inflight succeed. sample code + playbook link below." link: https://docs.redwood.ai/onboard/token-broker-pattern
ali: sample broker pattern (Node) we recommended to them:
```js
// broker caches identical ephemeral exchanges for 2s
const cache = new Map();
app.get('/broker/ephemeral', async (req, res)=>{
  const key = `ephemeral-${req.query.userId}`;
  const hit = cache.get(key);
  const now = Date.now();
  if (hit && now - hit.ts < 2000) return res.json(hit.token);
  const token = await exchangeLLForEphemeral(process.env.LL_KEY);
  cache.set(key, { token, ts: now });
  setTimeout(()=>cache.delete(key), 2100);
  res.json(token);
});
```
ali: sample Rust-friendly init wrapper we suggested to cap concurrency + backoff:
```rust
// pseudo-code
let sem = Semaphore::new(8);
async fn safe_init(client: &Client) -> Result<Response> {
  let _permit = sem.acquire().await;
  for i in 0..5 {
    match client.chat().create(&req).await {
      Ok(r) => return Ok(r),
      Err(e) if is_429(&e) => tokio::time::sleep(Duration::from_millis((1<<i)*100 + rand_jitter())).await,
      Err(e) => return Err(e),
    }
  }
  Err(anyhow!("init failed after retries"))
}
```
rahul: i'll enable rule rl-20260418-61:coalesce for account=solaris window=120s once CS gives green light. note: coalescing will reduce 429s but may slightly increase tail latency. we'll monitor traces.
